<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santal people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Santhal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Santal are one of the largest Adivasi peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. They speak Santali, a Munda language with its own Ol Chiki script. In 1855–1856 the Santal rebellion against landlords, moneylenders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> led to the creation of the Santal Parganas district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santal-people.jpg" alt="Santal people" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramjit Tudu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baha_parab_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Santal, also spelled Santhal, are an Austroasiatic-speaking Munda people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. They are the largest tribal community in the Indian states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, and are also present in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a>. They are the largest ethnic minority in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and have a sizeable population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Their ethnonym is Hor Hopon, child of human. The name Santal is most likely an exonym, derived from Saont in the Midnapore region of West Bengal, a word meaning plain land.</p><p>According to the linguist Paul Sidwell, speakers of Austroasiatic languages probably arrived on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> about 4,000 to 3,500 years ago and mixed extensively with local populations. The original homeland of the Santals is not known with certainty. Their folklore says they came from Hihiri, which scholars identify as Ahuri in the Hazaribagh district, and were pushed onto the Chhota Nagpur Plateau and finally to Saont.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Santals enter the historical record in 1795 as Soontars. The Bengal Famine of 1770 had depopulated the western and south-western parts of Bengal, especially the Jungle Mahals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> lost revenue. After the Permanent Settlement of 1790 the Company looked for agriculturalists to clear the land, and British officials turned to the Santals, who were ready to clear forest for settled agriculture. In 1832 a large area of the Raj Mahal hills was demarcated as Damin-i-koh, and Santals migrated there to cultivate it, taking loans from non-Santal moneylenders to buy iron tools, seed grain and oxen.</p><p>The community was split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> at Partition. After independence the Santals were made one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In East Pakistan and neighbouring West Bengal they supported the Tebhaga movement, and after the Pakistani military crushed the uprising and burned many Santal homes, many fled to Malda in India. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in 2000, the Santal Parganas became a separate division, which the Santals consider their cultural heartland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Santal society rests on a division between sibling, boeha, and guest, pera. Those in the closest brotherhood, mit orak hor, cannot marry each other, and clans are exogamous. A village council led by a manjhi handles local affairs. The principal festival is Sohrai, and dances are accompanied by two drums, the Tamak and the Tumdak. Santal marriage has seven recognised kinds, of which the most widely practised is kesimek.</p><p>The Santals worship nature in sacred groves called Jaher and Sarna, and many consider themselves followers of the Sari and Sarna religion rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. The Ol Chiki script of Santali was invented by Raghunath Murmu, known as Olguru, and Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu are remembered as leaders of the Santhal rebellion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santal people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mayurbhanj had a large <b>Santal</b>, Ho and Bhumij population and is the home of the Mayurbhanj Chhau dance.</blockquote><a href="/state/mayurbhanj-state">Mayurbhanj State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rajas ruled a population of Ho, <b>Santal</b> and Oriya-speaking peasants.</blockquote><a href="/state/seraikela-state">Seraikela State</a></li></ul></section>
